<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=pontoon-bridge">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pontoon bridge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/522-bce">522</a>–485 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as ponton bridge, floating bridge</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A pontoon bridge, also called a floating bridge, uses floats or shallow-draft boats to support a continuous deck for pedestrians and vehicles. Its load is limited by the buoyancy of the supports, so most are temporary and used in wartime and civil emergencies. The Greek Mandrocles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> engineered one about 2 kilometres long across the Bosporus for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> (522–485 BCE), and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> bridged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> in 480 BCE. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the first secure and permanent ones, linked with iron chains, came during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> (221–207 BCE).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pontoon-bridge.jpg" alt="Pontoon bridge" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US_Army_crossing_the_Rhine_on_heavy_ponton_bridge_at_Worms,_March,_1945.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>522–485 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Bosporus</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Mandrocles of Samos · <a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>China (9th or 8th century BCE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The Shi Jing records King Wen of Zhou building the first pontoon bridge in the 11th century BCE, but Joseph Needham holds that the temporary pontoon bridge was invented in China in the 9th or 8th century BCE, since that part of the text was probably added later. The Chhun Chhiu Hou Chuan places its invention in the Qin state in 257 BCE.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=pontoon-bridge">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
